    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d468">

                <pb facs="0668" n="568" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg568"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">251.11 Insull, Samuel/339: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-02-16T21:00:00+02:00">February 16, 1934—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 16—5:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>18. The Foreign Minister verbally requested me this afternoon to inform you that
                    his Government sincerely desires and intends to expel Insull but that owing to
                    the unusually inclement weather now prevailing and the fugitive’s physical
                    condition it believes it is only humane to allow him to remain from 10 days to 2
                    weeks longer and begs you to have patience. I asked Mr. Maximos<anchor n="29"
                        xml:id="d468-f1"/> if this meant that expulsion would then definitely take
                    place and he replied in the affirmative.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d468-f1" n="29" xml:id="d468-f1-content">
                <p>D. Maximos, Greek Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
